    "rationale": "Chose NO_FIX. The given scope (lines 170-180) contains only the p_cycle_counter register update; the path's startpoint IS that register, so all 0.94 ns of combinational delay lies downstream of it and outside this block. Two thirds of the delay (cells 1-8: NOR4/NAND4/OAI22/NOR2/AOI211/AND3/AND3/NAND3 = 0.62 ns) is the wide terminal-count decode of cycle_counter that produces the next_bit strobe and its FSM qualification - written elsewhere in uart_rx.v, not in my range. The remaining 0.24 ns (XOR2 x3 + NOR3) is the binary-to-Gray increment and full-compare inside wptr_full.v. RETIME rejected: the only register in scope is the path startpoint; there is no register sitting inside the combinational chain that could be moved forward, and the chain leaves the module. PIPELINE rejected: a cut on the next_bit strobe delays UART bit sampling by one cycle and reconverges with the FSM state register, the receive shift register and the FIFO write enable, all outside scope - that changes baud/data behaviour, not just latency; cutting in wptr_full is worse still, since the write pointer/full flag is a self-feedback loop whose latency is functionally observable. RESTRUCTURE rejected: the expensive decode is not textually in lines 170-180. The only restructure available inside my range would be re-encoding cycle_counter (down-count or one-hot) to cheapen the comparator, but cycle_counter is also compared against the half-bit value for start-bit sampling in code I was not shown, so re-encoding changes unseen behaviour. A correct fix is a latency-neutral restructure of the next_bit generation: compare cycle_counter against cycles_per_bit-1 and register that flag, so the strobe is a single flop output instead of an 8-level decode - identical assertion cycle because the counter increments unconditionally in FSM_START/RECV/STOP. That edit touches the next_bit declaration/assign, which is out of this scope.",
